        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVE:<br>
Prolonged spaceflight and mission operations expose long-duration astronauts to a unique set of risk factors that contribute to a decline in bone strength (both bone mass and quality). Some of these factors (e.g., sub-optimal nutrition, disuse, gender) are known to contribute to osteoporosis here on earth. However, some putative factors (e.g., radiation, hypercapnia, mission duration) are novel and need further research to understand the contribution to skeletal changes and to the overall osteoporosis risk itself. The primary contributor to skeletal changes during spaceflight is presumably the reduced mechanical load to the skeleton (skeletal disuse) due both to weightlessness and muscle atrophy. Additionally, space travel introduces an array of risk factors (perturbed vitamin D metabolism, reduced exercise activity, reduced calcium absorption, sarcopenia) that are induced because of mission operations in the extreme space environment. Osteoporosis is a syndrome with multiple pathophysiologies. With the large number of risk factors for bone loss during spaceflight it would be critical to understand which risk factors would be most critical for mitigation. This study addresses the Risk Of Early Onset Osteoporosis Due To Spaceflight, which requires prioritization of risk factors to focus countermeasure development, that is, the contribution of each risk factor on bone loss and recovery of bone strength, and which factors are the best targets for countermeasure application.<p>

Specifically, the aims of this study are to: 1) Determine evolution of changes in sclerostin IHC expression (Scl-IHC) in osteocytes and osteoblast-driven bone formation in 3 separate long bone compartments of young adult rats during 90 days of hindlimb suspension (HLS) and during the subsequent 90 days weightbearing recovery;  2) Determine if these responses vary by sex over 90 days of HU and 90 days of  recovery by determining the same outcomes in age-matched females; and 3) Determine if these responses change with age by determining the same outcomes in old-adult rats.  The results will guide the development of proposed therapeutic use of sclerostin antibody for maintaining bone integrity on long-duration missions.<p>

APPROACH:<br>
This project takes advantage of a unique biospecimen-sharing opportunity coordinated with Dr. Charles Fuller of UC-Davis via the NASA HERO mechanism.   Dr. Fuller is conducting long-term hindlimb unloading experiments (90 days) in young adult rodents of both sexes and in old adult male rats; because a small number of animals will be terminated at multiple time points, we can define the evolution of changes during early and late HU, as well as during a 90-day recovery period.   Tibial bone collected before and  after 4, 7 , 28 and 90 days of HU and after 4, 7 , 28 and 90 days of recovery will be stored for later shipment in batches to Texas A&M University.   Hence, the timeline and total subject numbers for data collection for this project depends fully on the execution of live animal experiments in the UC-Davis laboratory.   Their current plan is to provide tissues from 3 HU and weightbearing control rats at each time point; hence the anticipated total sample number is  (5 rats/time point x 9 timepoints = ) 45.<p>

Once tissues are received at Texas A&M, tibial bone will be prepared histologically for analysis of two major outcomes:  1) immunohistochemistry (IHC) staining of osteocytes (the most common bone cell type, embedded inside bone) for sclerostin and 2) histomorphometric indices of bone formation on bone surfaces.   Sclerostin is a key regulator of bone formation activity, signaling up- or down-regulation of bone formation activity to bone surface cells, and its expression by osteocytes varies with mechanical loading (or unloading).  A unique aspect of our approach is to create sections that allow for simultaneous evaluation of osteocyte sclerostin in 3 different bone compartments:  1) proximal tibia metaphysis cancellous bone , 2) metaphyseal cortical bone, and 3) mid-shaft diaphyseal cortical bone.   Hence we can verify if earlier results demonstrating that mRNA for sclerostin increases with HU in mid-shaft cortical bone, but decreases with HU in metaphyseal cancellous bone translates to similar trends for sclerostin protein.    Standard histomorphometric measures of osteoblast and osteoid surfaces (indices of bone formation activity) in cancellous and cortical bone compartments will be quantified in all samples to verify if altered sclerostin levels exert the same biological effect in these different bone sites.<p>

Results:<br>
This experiment is currently in progress.  Results will be available at the conclusion of the study.
